faq:
  - q: "Do we really need a policy? We're only ten people."
    a: "Ten people is exactly where it pays off, because informal norms don't survive a busy week. It needn't be formal — one page pinned where people work is a policy. The alternative isn't 'no policy', it's ten different personal ones."
  - q: "Should we just ban AI to be safe?"
    a: "Bans reliably move usage somewhere you can't see rather than stopping it, because the productivity gain is real and staff are under pressure. You end up with the same risk minus any visibility. Approving a tool and setting limits is the safer position."
  - q: "Who should write it?"
    a: "Someone who understands the work, with input from whoever owns risk. It shouldn't be drafted by a person who has never used the tools — that's how you get rules that are impossible to follow and get ignored on day one."
